<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

"How many refunds has each organization approved today, and what is the largest?" You could compute it with an aggregate on every page load. At enterprise volume you would rather keep a **projection**: a small table updated from the event log, which can be thrown away and rebuilt from the log at any time.

`ash_events_projections` gives you three pieces, and each has one job:

| Piece | Job | Rule |
|---|---|---|
| **Projection resource** | the *shape*: one row per **grain** | no business logic in it |
| **Projector** | the *rule*: event in, ops out | the only writer |
| **Engine** | checkpoints, advisory locks, DLQ, rebuild | at-least-once, idempotent |

## One log per resource, and which log the engine reads

A resource carries exactly one `events do event_log ... end`, and in this app every platform resource already points at the tamper-evident `AshEnterprise.Audit.EventLog` (lesson 15). The projection engine drains a *different* log, `AshEnterprise.Compliance.EventLog`, configured once:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
config :ash_events_projections,
  repo: AshEnterprise.Repo,
  pubsub: AshEnterprise.PubSub,
  event_log: AshEnterprise.Compliance.EventLog,
  event_table: "compliance_events",
  projectors: [AshEnterprise.Compliance.Projector],
  start_projectors?: true,
  start_probe?: true
```

Why two logs? The audit log's schema belongs to the hash chain and must never be pruned; the consumable log is shaped for its readers (`organization_id` is a real column, stamped from metadata) and wakes projectors on insert. So events meant for consumers are **appended explicitly** by a change on the action that produces them. That append is still declared on the action, so it happens for every caller.

## Step 1: append a consumable event

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
# lib/ash_enterprise/tutorial/changes/record_refund_event.ex
defmodule AshEnterprise.Tutorial.Changes.RecordRefundEvent do
  use Ash.Resource.Change

  @impl true
  def change(changeset, opts, _ctx) do
    Ash.Changeset.after_action(changeset, fn _cs, refund ->
      AshEnterprise.Compliance.EventLog
      |> Ash.Changeset.for_create(:create, %{
        resource: AshEnterprise.Tutorial.Refund,
        record_id: refund.id,
        action: opts[:as],
        action_type: :update,
        data: %{"amount" => Decimal.to_string(refund.amount), "status" => to_string(refund.status)},
        metadata: %{"organization_id" => refund.organization_id},
        occurred_at: DateTime.utc_now()
      })
      |> Ash.create!(actor: AshEnterprise.Platform.SystemActor.projector())

      {:ok, refund}
    end)
  end
end
```

Add `change {AshEnterprise.Tutorial.Changes.RecordRefundEvent, as: :refund_approved}` to the Refund's `:approve` action. (Look up the exact system actor function your checkout exposes with `mix ash_agent.search SystemActor`; do not guess.)

## Step 2: the shape

Write `AshEnterprise.Tutorial.RefundStats` with the `AshEvents.Projections.ProjectionResource` extension, grain `[:organization_id, :day]`, and integer/decimal fields `approved_count` (default 0), `approved_total` (default 0) and `largest` (nullable). Do **not** declare an identity.

**Enter your solution**

```elixir

```

<details class="rounded-lg my-4" style="background-color: #96ef86; color: #040604;">
  <summary class="cursor-pointer font-bold p-4"></i>Show Solution</summary>
  <div class="p-4">

  ```elixir
  defmodule AshEnterprise.Tutorial.RefundStats do
    use Ash.Resource,
      domain: AshEnterprise.Tutorial,
      data_layer: AshPostgres.DataLayer,
      extensions: [AshEvents.Projections.ProjectionResource]

    postgres do
      table "tutorial_refund_stats"
      repo AshEnterprise.Repo
    end

    projection_resource do
      grain_fields [:organization_id, :day]
    end

    attributes do
      uuid_primary_key :id
      attribute :organization_id, :uuid, allow_nil?: false, public?: true
      attribute :day, :date, allow_nil?: false, public?: true
      attribute :approved_count, :integer, default: 0, public?: true
      attribute :approved_total, :decimal, default: 0, public?: true
      attribute :largest, :decimal, public?: true
    end

    actions do
      defaults [:read]
    end
  end
  ```

  The transformer derives the `:by_grain` identity from `grain_fields`. Declaring it yourself is a mistake.
  Register it in the domain, then `mix ash.codegen tutorial_refund_stats && mix ash.migrate`.

  </div>
</details>

## Step 3: the rule

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
# lib/ash_enterprise/tutorial/refund_projector.ex
defmodule AshEnterprise.Tutorial.RefundProjector do
  use AshEvents.Projections.Projector,
    name: "tutorial_refund_stats_v1",
    event_log: AshEnterprise.Compliance.EventLog,
    projection_resource: AshEnterprise.Tutorial.RefundStats

  grain fn event ->
    %{organization_id: event.organization_id, day: DateTime.to_date(event.occurred_at)}
  end

  project AshEnterprise.Tutorial.Refund, :refund_approved, fn event ->
    amount = Decimal.new(event.data["amount"])

    [
      {:increment, :approved_count, 1},
      {:increment, :approved_total, amount},
      {:max, :largest, amount}
    ]
  end
end
```

Add it to `projectors:` in config and restart the node. Points to notice:

* The **name is versioned** (`_v1`). Changing the rule means a `_v2` built side by side, then a switch: a blue/green deploy for read models.
* The handler is **arity 1**: stateless, ops only. Arity 2 receives the current row, for rules that need it.
* Return `nil` to skip an event.

## Use it

Approve two refunds (lesson 15), then read the projection. It is a resource, so:

**Enter your solution**

```elixir

```

<details class="rounded-lg my-4" style="background-color: #96ef86; color: #040604;">
  <summary class="cursor-pointer font-bold p-4"></i>Show Solution</summary>
  <div class="p-4">

  ```elixir
  AshEnterprise.Tutorial.RefundStats
  |> Ash.read!(actor: actor, tenant: tenant)
  ```

  If the row is missing, debug in order: did the event land (`Ash.read!(AshEnterprise.Compliance.EventLog, ...)` filtered by `record_id`)? Is the projector lagging or dead-lettered (`mix ash_events_projections.lag`)? Only then read the projector code.

  </div>
</details>

## Operations you must know

```bash
mix ash_events_projections.lag                                     # lag + DLQ snapshot
mix ash_events_projections.verify                                  # assertion-style sync check
mix ash_events_projections.rebuild --projection=tutorial_refund_stats_v1   # truncate + replay
mix ash_events_projections.dlq inspect --projection=tutorial_refund_stats_v1
```

Never use `reset` in place of `rebuild`: clearing the checkpoint without truncating makes `:increment` ops double-count. And never write to `tutorial_refund_stats` from app code; the projector and its rebuilder are the only writers.

## Exercise: attach it to the parent

Show `approved_count` for the refund's organization and day directly on a Refund. Use `AshEvents.Projections.AttachProjection` rather than a hand-written join.

<details class="rounded-lg my-4" style="background-color: #96ef86; color: #040604;">
  <summary class="cursor-pointer font-bold p-4"></i>Show Solution</summary>
  <div class="p-4">

  ```elixir
  # on Refund: extra_extensions: [AshEvents.Projections.AttachProjection]
  attach_projection AshEnterprise.Tutorial.RefundProjector do
    field :approved_today, :approved_count, default: 0
  end
  ```

  The projector must implement `current_grain_for_record/1` so the parent knows which grain row is "its" row.

  </div>
</details>

